# Drafted by Gemini (R11 batch b5), edited. mori_shade's first conversation.
# Bringing back the fallen (raising..raising_closed): drafted by Gemini (docs/plans/gemini_raise_dead_mori/), the seven lines as drafted. The sum is the engine's
# raise_dead service on Mori's NPC data (data/npcs/mori_shade.json: a flat 1,000 gp for each hero, no diamond, 07:00 to 23:00); the
# services screen shows it for each hero before anything is paid, and it also opens from her menu on the map (Services). These lines are not voiced yet.

~ start
set mori_shade_met
Narrator: Dried rosemary and fresh peat cling to the woman in black, her white hair gleaming like bone.
mori_shade [neutral]: Step softly, newly arrived. This ground has welcomed many, and will hold many more before the moon wanes.
-> menu

~ menu
* Ask about her work as undertaker -> about_work
* Ask about the faiths of Wickermoor -> about_village
* Ask about burial and a proper rest -> shop_open
* Ask whether one of ours who has fallen can be called back -> raising_gate
* Ask for a piece of old valley lore -> rumour
* [Religion DC 13] Ask about the ferryman's token at her collar -> check_ok | check_fail
* Take our leave -> END

~ about_work
mori_shade [neutral]: Druskenvald's soil does not easily let the fallen go. I wash the brow, fold the hands, and pay the earth its due.
mori_shade [weary]: Old Yorgrim at the cemetery taught me the spade-work. Every soul deserves a quiet pillow.
-> menu

~ about_village
mori_shade [neutral]: The Mayor's folk feed the Oak of Many Faces blood, and the Brightsingers sing at the dark. We Strangers just owe the Ferryman a nod and a clean ledger.
-> menu

~ shop_open
mori_shade [neutral]: Tell me whose memory we are tending, and we shall see them properly laid to rest.
-> menu

~ rumour
mori_shade [sad]: Do not pluck the white violets round the Founder's Round. Their roots drink from the nameless dead, and weep on your fingers.
-> menu

~ check_ok
Narrator: She touches the tarnished brass oar pinned to her lapel, her voice dropping to the hush of river reeds.
mori_shade [smile]: You know the Vagrant's mark. He rowed us across the dark water once, and watches to see if we waste our second breath.
-> menu

~ check_fail
mori_shade [neutral]: Just a riverbank keepsake, traveller. A reminder that every road ends at the water's edge.
-> menu

# Bringing back one of the party who has truly died: within the day's hours Mori states the terms and opens her services (the sum for each
# hero, the dead first); outside them she says when to come back. The offer stands either way (nothing is spent by asking).
~ raising_gate
if hour >= 7 and hour < 23
-> raising
endif
-> raising_closed

~ raising
Narrator: The parlor goes awful still save for dry pages rustlin' as she opens a black ledger smellin' of cedar and cold tallow.
mori_shade [neutral]: I do not call the dead back myself. I only carry the Ferryman his toll and ask him to turn his oar.
mori_shade [sad]: He turns it only for souls dead ten days or less, and they return weak for ten days with the water's shadow on them.
mori_shade [neutral]: The toll is a thousand gold pieces each, whoever they were. I ask no other payment.
mori_shade [neutral]: You will see each companion's toll before paying. Tell me whom to call back to the near shore.
services
-> raising_after

~ raising_after
mori_shade [neutral]: The Ferryman's ledger stays open for the full ten days, whenever your purse allows.
-> menu

~ raising_closed
Narrator: The lamps burn low in the parlor, and a damp chill settles deep into the quiet room at this hour.
mori_shade [weary]: The Midnight and Witching hours belong to the graves. The Ferryman takes no asking now.
mori_shade [neutral]: Come back between the Twilight and Evening hours. The ten days will keep.
-> menu
